9.theory:
## Syntax

```js
if (x > 0) {
  console.log("+");
} else if (x < 0) {
  console.log("-");
} else {
  console.log("0");
}
```

Condition in `()`, body in `{}`. Like Java.

## Operators

`==` (don't), `===`, `!==`, `<`, `>`, `<=`, `>=`.

## Logical

`&&` — and, `||` — or, `!` — not.

```js
if (age >= 18 && hasPassport) { }
if (name === "Anna" || name === "Ivan") { }
```

## Ternary

```js
let s = x > 0 ? "positive" : "negative";
```

`condition ? if_true : if_false`.

## Truthy/falsy in condition

```js
if (name) { }
```

`name = ""` — false, `name = "Anna"` — true. Not always obvious — sometimes better `if (name !== "")`.

## What goes wrong

- `if (x = 5)` — `=` assignment, not error. `x` becomes `5`, condition truthy. Use `===`.
- `if (x > 0);` — `;` cuts the body.

## Common mistake

`if (name == "Anna")` — `==`. Use `===`.

## Summary

- `if (condition) { }` — parens and braces
- `&&`, `||`, `!`
- `a ? b : c` — ternary
- `===` instead of `==`
- Truthy/falsy in condition

