11.theory:
## for

```js
for (let i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
  console.log(i);
}
```

Three parts via `;`: init, condition, step.

## while

```js
let i = 1;
while (i <= 5) {
  console.log(i);
  i++;
}
```

Checks **before** body.

## do-while

```js
let i = 1;
do {
  console.log(i);
  i++;
} while (i <= 5);
```

Checks **after** — runs at least once.

## Nested

```js
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  for (let j = 0; j < 2; j++) {
    console.log(i, j);
  }
}
```

## What goes wrong

- Forget `i++` — infinite loop.
- `let i` in `for` — scope **inside the loop**.

## Common mistake

`for (var i = ...)` — `i` leaks out. Use `let`.

## Summary

- `for (init; cond; step)`
- `while (cond)`
- `do { } while (cond);`
- Without step — infinite
- `let i` in `for` — block scope

12.theory:
## break — exit

```js
for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
  if (i === 3) break;
  console.log(i);
}
```

Output: `0 1 2`.

## continue — skip iteration

```js
for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
  if (i === 2) continue;
  console.log(i);
}
```

Output: `0 1 3 4`.

## Difference

| `break` | `continue` |
|---|---|
| Exits **entirely** | Skips **one** iteration |

## break in nested

```js
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  for (let j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
    if (j === 1) break;
    console.log(i, j);
  }
}
```

Exits only **inner**.

## while True + break

```js
while (true) {
  let cmd = "exit";
  if (cmd === "exit") break;
}
```

Classic pattern.

## What goes wrong

- `break` outside loop — SyntaxError.
- `continue` in `switch` — works but confusing.

## Common mistake

Thinking `break` exits all loops. Only current.

## Summary

- `break` — exit
- `continue` — skip iteration
- `break` in nested — only inner
- `while (true) + break` — standard

