22.theory:
## Class

```js
class Animal {
  constructor(name) {
    this.name = name;
  }

  speak() {
    console.log(`${this.name} makes a sound`);
  }
}

const a = new Animal("Rex");
a.speak();   // Rex makes a sound
```

- `class` — keyword
- `constructor` — called on `new`
- `this.name = name` — store field
- Method — just a function inside, no `function` keyword

## Inheritance

```js
class Dog extends Animal {
  speak() {
    console.log(`${this.name}: Woof!`);
  }
}

const d = new Dog("Rex");
d.speak();   // Rex: Woof!
```

`extends` — inherits fields and methods. Override — just redeclare.

## super

```js
class Dog extends Animal {
  constructor(name, breed) {
    super(name);          // parent constructor
    this.breed = breed;
  }
}
```

`super(...)` — call parent constructor. Must be **first line** in child constructor.

## Getters / setters

```js
class Circle {
  constructor(r) { this.r = r; }
  get area() { return Math.PI * this.r ** 2; }
}
const c = new Circle(2);
console.log(c.area);   // 12.566
```

`get` — property-like method.

## Static

```js
class Math2 {
  static square(x) { return x * x; }
}
console.log(Math2.square(4));   // 16
```

`static` — on class, not instance.

## Under the hood

Classes are syntactic sugar over prototypes. But use `class` — clearer and safer.

## What goes wrong

- Forgetting `new` — `TypeError: cannot call class without new`.
- `this` lost when method passed — use `bind` or arrow field.
- Forget `super()` in child with constructor — `ReferenceError`.

## Common mistake

`const a = Animal("Rex")` without `new`. Always `new Animal(...)`.

## Summary

- `class Name { constructor() {} method() {} }`
- `new Name(...)`
- `extends` — inherit
- `super(...)` — call parent
- `get` / `set` — properties
- `static` — on class

26.theory:
## Creation

```js
const p = document.createElement("p");
p.textContent = "New paragraph";
```

`p` — new element, not in DOM yet.

## Insertion

```js
parent.appendChild(p);
```

`appendChild` — at end.

## Others

```js
parent.append(p, text);
parent.prepend(p);
parent.before(p);
parent.after(p);
```

## Removal

```js
el.remove();
parent.removeChild(el);
```

## Build list

```js
const ul = document.querySelector("#list");
["Milk", "Bread", "Eggs"].forEach(text => {
  const li = document.createElement("li");
  li.textContent = text;
  ul.appendChild(li);
});
```

## Why not innerHTML

`innerHTML +=` recreates everything, loses listeners. `createElement` + `append` — targeted.

## What goes wrong

- Insert element twice — moves, not copies.
- `appendChild(null)` — TypeError.

## Common mistake

`parent.appendChild("<p>x</p>")` — `appendChild` takes **element**, not string. String — via `innerHTML`.

## Summary

- `createElement("p")` — create
- `parent.appendChild(el)` — insert
- `el.remove()` — delete
- Don't confuse with `innerHTML`

28.theory:
## submit

Form submission event:

```js
const form = document.querySelector("form");
form.addEventListener("submit", (event) => {
  event.preventDefault();
  console.log("submit intercepted");
});
```

By default submit **reloads** page. `preventDefault()` cancels.

## preventDefault

```js
event.preventDefault();
```

Stops default action. For clicks — link navigation, for forms — submission.

## Field value

```js
const input = document.querySelector("#name");
const value = input.value;
```

`value` — what's typed.

## Example

```js
form.addEventListener("submit", (e) => {
  e.preventDefault();
  const text = input.value;
  output.textContent = text;
});
```

## reset

```js
form.reset();
input.value = "";
```

## input

Event `input` — on every change:

```js
input.addEventListener("input", () => {
  console.log(input.value);
});
```

## What goes wrong

- Forget `preventDefault` — page reloads.
- `e.preventDefault` without `()` — not called.

## Common mistake

`submit` without `preventDefault` — reload. Always cancel.

## Summary

- `submit` — on form
- `preventDefault()` — cancel
- `input.value` — value
- `form.reset()` — clear
- `input` — on every change

29.theory:
## keydown

```js
document.addEventListener("keydown", (event) => {
  console.log(event.key);
});
```

Fires on key press.

## event.key

- `"a"` — letter
- `"Enter"` — Enter
- `"Escape"` — Esc
- `"ArrowUp"` / `"ArrowDown"` — arrows
- `" "` — space

## Modifiers

- `event.code` — physical code (`"KeyA"`, `"Space"`)
- `event.ctrlKey` / `shiftKey` / `altKey` — true/false

```js
document.addEventListener("keydown", (e) => {
  if (e.ctrlKey && e.key === "s") {
    e.preventDefault();
    console.log("save intercepted");
  }
});
```

## keyup

```js
document.addEventListener("keyup", (e) => {
  console.log("released", e.key);
});
```

## What goes wrong

- `event.key` for Shift — `"Shift"`.
- `preventDefault` on all keydown — breaks Tab, arrows.

## Common mistake

`event.keyCode` — deprecated. Use `event.key`.

## Summary

- `keydown` — press
- `event.key` — char or name
- `ctrlKey` / `shiftKey` — modifiers
- `keyCode` — deprecated

30.theory:
## What we build

Counter with + and − buttons. We apply: DOM, events, text update.

## HTML

```html
<button id="dec">−</button>
<span id="value">0</span>
<button id="inc">+</button>
```

## JS

```js
let count = 0;
const value = document.querySelector("#value");
const inc = document.querySelector("#inc");
const dec = document.querySelector("#dec");

inc.addEventListener("click", () => {
  count++;
  value.textContent = count;
});

dec.addEventListener("click", () => {
  count--;
  value.textContent = count;
});
```

## Checks

- `count` — variable
- `textContent` — updated
- `addEventListener` on both buttons
- `++` / `--`

## What goes wrong

- `value.textContent = count++` — shows old, then ++.
- Forget to update `textContent` — number doesn't change.

## Common mistake

`count = value.textContent + 1` — string concat. Use `Number(...)` or numeric `count`.

## Summary

- `let count` — state
- `addEventListener` on buttons
- `textContent` — update
- `count++` / `count--`

31.theory:
## What it is

`localStorage` — key-value storage in the browser. Persists between page reloads and sessions. ~5 MB limit.

## API

```js
localStorage.setItem("key", "value");
localStorage.getItem("key");     // value or null
localStorage.removeItem("key");
localStorage.clear();            // wipe all
```

## Always strings

`localStorage` stores **strings only**. Numbers and objects must be serialized:

```js
localStorage.setItem("count", "5");
let n = Number(localStorage.getItem("count"));
```

## Objects and arrays

Use JSON:

```js
const user = { name: "Anna", age: 25 };
localStorage.setItem("user", JSON.stringify(user));

const raw = localStorage.getItem("user");
const parsed = raw ? JSON.parse(raw) : null;
console.log(parsed.name);   // Anna
```

## Persisting counter

```js
let count = Number(localStorage.getItem("count")) || 0;
value.textContent = count;

inc.addEventListener("click", () => {
  count++;
  value.textContent = count;
  localStorage.setItem("count", count);
});
```

`|| 0` — fallback if `null`.

## sessionStorage

Same API, but cleared when the tab closes.

## What goes wrong

- `localStorage.setItem("x", 42)` — stored as `"42"`.
- `JSON.parse(null)` — throws. Guard with `?`.
- Storing large objects — quota exceeded.

## Common mistake

Forgetting `JSON.stringify` — objects become `"[object Object]"`.

## Summary

- `setItem/getItem/removeItem/clear`
- Strings only
- `JSON.stringify` / `JSON.parse` for objects
- `sessionStorage` — per tab

