32.theory:
## What event loop is

JS is **single-threaded**, but handles multiple things "at once" via event loop. Sync code runs immediately, async code is queued.

## setTimeout

```js
setTimeout(() => {
  console.log("After 2 seconds");
}, 2000);
```

First arg — function, second — delay (ms).

## Order

```js
console.log("1");
setTimeout(() => console.log("3"), 0);
console.log("2");
// 1, 2, 3 — not 1, 3, 2
```

Even setTimeout with 0 is deferred: sync code first.

## clearTimeout

```js
const id = setTimeout(() => console.log("won't fire"), 1000);
clearTimeout(id);
```

## setInterval

```js
const id = setInterval(() => console.log("tick"), 1000);
// clearInterval(id); — stop
```

Every N ms.

## How event loop works

1. Sync code runs to completion.
2. Then microtasks (Promise, queueMicrotask).
3. Then macrotasks (setTimeout, setInterval, events).
4. Repeat from step 2.

```js
console.log("1");
setTimeout(() => console.log("3"), 0);
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log("2"));
// 1, 2, 3 — microtasks before macrotasks
```

## What goes wrong

- `setTimeout` with 0 — still async.
- Forget `clearInterval` — leak (timer runs forever).

## Common mistake

Expect `setTimeout(fn, 0)` to run before next line. No, only after sync code.

## Summary

- JS single-threaded, but has event loop
- `setTimeout(fn, ms)` — after N ms
- `setInterval(fn, ms)` — every N ms
- Sync first, then micro, then macro
- `clearTimeout` / `clearInterval`

34.theory:
## What async is

`async` makes a function asynchronous — always returns a Promise:

```js
async function greet() {
  return "Hello";
}
greet().then(r => console.log(r));  // Hello
```

## await

Inside `async` — `await` waits for a Promise:

```js
async function main() {
  const result = await fetchData();
  console.log(result);
}
```

Code after `await` runs when Promise resolves.

## try / catch

```js
async function main() {
  try {
    const data = await fetchData();
    console.log(data);
  } catch (err) {
    console.log("Error:", err.message);
  }
}
```

`await` on rejected Promise throws.

## Sequential await

```js
const a = await fetchA();  // wait
const b = await fetchB();  // wait
```

One after another. If independent — use `Promise.all`:

```js
const [a, b] = await Promise.all([fetchA(), fetchB()]);
```

Parallel — faster.

## await outside async

Only in top-level modules (ES2022). In regular script — syntax error.

## Difference from .then

- `.then` — chains, `.catch`
- `async/await` — looks like sync code, easier to read

Same under the hood.

## What goes wrong

- `await` without `async` — SyntaxError.
- Forget try/catch around await — error bubbles up.

## Common mistake

`await` in `for...of` loop — waits each. In `Promise.all` — parallel. Choose by task.

## Summary

- `async function` — returns Promise
- `await` — waits
- `try / catch` — catches
- `Promise.all` — parallel
- Simpler than `.then`

35.theory:
## What fetch is

`fetch` — built-in for HTTP requests. Returns a Promise.

```js
fetch("https://api.example.com/data")
  .then(response => response.json())
  .then(data => console.log(data))
  .catch(err => console.log(err));
```

## Response

`fetch` returns `Response` object:

- `response.ok` — `true` if 200–299
- `response.status` — code (200, 404, 500)
- `response.json()` — parse JSON, returns Promise
- `response.text()` — text

## Status check

```js
fetch(url).then(res => {
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error("HTTP " + res.status);
  return res.json();
});
```

`fetch` **does not throw** on 404/500 — only on network errors. Check `res.ok`.

## async / await

```js
async function loadData() {
  try {
    const response = await fetch(url);
    if (!response.ok) throw new Error("HTTP " + response.status);
    const data = await response.json();
    console.log(data);
  } catch (err) {
    console.log("Error:", err.message);
  }
}
```

## POST

```js
fetch(url, {
  method: "POST",
  headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
  body: JSON.stringify({ name: "Anna" })
});
```

## What goes wrong

- Forget `response.json()` — get Response, not data.
- Only `.catch` without `res.ok` — 404 passes as success.
- CORS policy may block.

## Common mistake

Thinking `fetch` throws on 404. It doesn't. Check `res.ok`.

## Summary

- `fetch(url)` — Promise
- `res.json()` / `res.text()`
- `res.ok` — status check
- `fetch` doesn't throw on 4xx/5xx
- POST — `method`, `headers`, `body`

37.theory:
## What we build

Adding tasks via input + button. Everything together: DOM, createElement, appendChild, events, forms, localStorage.

## HTML

```html
<input id="inp" type="text">
<button id="add">Add</button>
<ul id="list"></ul>
```

## JS

```js
const inp = document.querySelector("#inp");
const add = document.querySelector("#add");
const list = document.querySelector("#list");

add.addEventListener("click", () => {
  const text = inp.value.trim();
  if (!text) return;

  const li = document.createElement("li");
  li.textContent = text;
  list.appendChild(li);

  inp.value = "";
});
```

## Breakdown

- `querySelector` — find
- `addEventListener` — on button
- `inp.value.trim()` — value without spaces
- `if (!text) return` — skip empty
- `createElement("li")` — create
- `appendChild` — append
- `inp.value = ""` — clear

## Persisting with localStorage

```js
const saved = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem("tasks") || "[]");
saved.forEach(text => {
  const li = document.createElement("li");
  li.textContent = text;
  list.appendChild(li);
});

add.addEventListener("click", () => {
  const text = inp.value.trim();
  if (!text) return;
  const li = document.createElement("li");
  li.textContent = text;
  list.appendChild(li);
  const all = [...list.children].map(c => c.textContent);
  localStorage.setItem("tasks", JSON.stringify(all));
  inp.value = "";
});
```

## Checks

- Event on button
- Create + insert
- Clear input
- Skip empty
- Persist via localStorage

## What goes wrong

- Don't clear `inp.value` — can add same thing many times.
- Don't check empty — empty `<li>`.
- Forget `JSON.stringify` — saves `[object Object]`.

## Common mistake

`inp.value += " "` — accumulates text. Need `inp.value = ""`.

## Summary

- `querySelector` + `addEventListener` + `createElement`
- `trim()` — remove spaces
- `if (!text) return` — skip
- Clear after add
- `localStorage` for persistence
